From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 +0000
From: NebulaAGI <gyccc@atomcode.com>
Date: Wed, 6 Aug 2025 04:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] build.xml test target: propagate JAXP/socket-timeout
 sysproperties to the forked JUnit JVM

The upstream `build.xml` test target uses `<junit fork="true">`
with the Ant default `forkmode="perTest"`, so every test method
gets a freshly forked JUnit JVM.  Ant's `<junit fork="true">`
does **not** automatically inherit the parent Ant JVM's `-D`
system properties into the forked JVM — only the `<sysproperty>`
child elements of `<junit>` are passed to the fork JVM command
line via `CommandlineJava.SysProperties.setSystem()`.

The xom recipe previously launched `ant test` with

    -Djavax.xml.accessExternalDTD=none
    -Dsun.net.client.defaultConnectTimeout=5000
    -Dsun.net.client.defaultReadTimeout=5000

on the Ant command line.  These three properties were set on
the **parent Ant JVM only** and never reached the forked JUnit
JVM that actually runs `nu.xom.tests.BuilderTest` et al.  The
fork JVM therefore kept doing real network I/O for every
`builder.build("http://...")` call (BuilderTest.testChemistry
pulls a remote DTD from rsc.org; several BuilderTest/
BaseURITest/IDTest/XPathTest cases fetch remote XML documents
whose DTDs themselves pull further external entities).  On the
HarmonyOS PC preflight machine the forked JVMs accumulate
network sockets / Xerces entity cache state until the whole
`ant test` process is SIGKILL'd (exit code 137 = 128 +
SIGKILL), typically at `BuilderTest` after `BaseURITest`'s
79 URI tests have already run.

Fix: add three `<sysproperty>` child elements to the upstream
`<junit fork="true">` block in the `test` target so Ant
propagates them to the forked JUnit JVM:

  1. `javax.xml.accessExternalDTD=none` — JAXP 1.5+ standard
     property (Xerces 2.12.2 honors it) that makes the parser
     refuse to load external DTD subsets, so testChemistry's
     remote DTD fetch fails fast with an IOException instead of
     hanging on rsc.org.

  2. `sun.net.client.defaultConnectTimeout=5000` — JRE global
     connect timeout (5 s) applying to every HttpURLConnection
     in the fork JVM.

  3. `sun.net.client.defaultReadTimeout=5000` — JRE global read
     timeout (5 s), the backstop for cases
     `accessExternalDTD=none` does not cover (BuilderTest/
     BaseURITest/IDTest/XPathTest directly fetching remote XML
     documents).  Any stuck TCP read raises
     `java.net.SocketTimeoutException` within 5 s instead of
     blocking forever.

With these three properties actually present in the fork JVM,
the already-applied `xom-1.4.4-ci-network.patch` widening of
`CITestUtil.isNetworkException` (to recognise
`SocketTimeoutException` and HTTP 4xx/5xx bare IOExceptions)
and `isRunningInCI` (to recognise `HMBS_INSTALL`) takes effect:
network-dependent upstream tests hit an IOException /
SocketTimeoutException, the upstream `shouldIgnore(ex)` guard
fires, and the test is skipped as upstream intended — no
assertion is removed or weakened.

This is a portability patch (test-infrastructure only): it
changes how the test JVM is launched, not the library or the
test assertions.

---
 build.xml | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/build.xml b/build.xml
index 9d62844..41e0115 100644
--- a/build.xml
+++ b/build.xml
@@ -567,6 +567,9 @@ XOM Build file
     <junit fork="true" jvm="${jvm}" failureproperty="testsfailed">
        <classpath refid="test.class.path" />
        <sysproperty key="jaxen.data.dir" value="${build.dir}/jaxen-${jaxenversion}"/>
+       <sysproperty key="javax.xml.accessExternalDTD" value="none"/>
+       <sysproperty key="sun.net.client.defaultConnectTimeout" value="5000"/>
+       <sysproperty key="sun.net.client.defaultReadTimeout" value="5000"/>
        <formatter type="${test.outputFormat}" />
        <formatter type="brief" usefile="false" />
        <batchtest fork="true" todir="${testoutput.dir}">
